            <ArticleTitle>Detection of virus particles resembling circovirus and porcine circovirus 2a (PCV2a) sequences in feces of dogs.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>51-53</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0034-5288(17)30069-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.rvsc.2017.01.014</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>During routine electron microscopy of fecal samples from diarrheic dogs dated from 2000 virus particles resembling circovirus in shape and size were detected in two samples (V2177/00; V3374/00). Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) using primers specific for porcine circovirus type 2 (PCV2) amplified DNA recovered from both samples. Sequencing of PCR amplificates (V2177/00) obtained with PCV2-specific primer pairs revealed a genome size of 1768bp. The nucleotide sequence was highly similar (98% nucleotide identity) to the PCV2a reference sequence.</AbstractText>
